= Knowledge =

== Overview ==
'''Knowledge''' is an epistemic state or achievement in which a subject gets at the truth in an appropriate way. The concept is central to [[Epistemology|epistemology]], which asks what knowledge is, how it is acquired, how it differs from mere [[Belief|belief]], and what limits it.

The ordinary word "know" covers several different constructions. Philosophical analysis has focused especially on '''propositional knowledge'''—knowing that something is the case—but knowledge-how and knowledge by acquaintance are also important.

== Major forms ==
'''Propositional knowledge''' is usually expressed as "S knows that p", where a subject knows a proposition.

'''Knowledge-how''' concerns abilities or ways of doing something, such as knowing how to ride a bicycle. Philosophers disagree about whether knowledge-how can be reduced to propositional knowledge.

'''Knowledge by acquaintance''' or familiarity concerns knowing a person, place, object, or experience directly rather than merely knowing propositions about it.

These forms overlap in practice but should not be treated as interchangeable by definition.

== Knowledge and belief ==
Knowledge is closely connected to [[Belief]]. If a person knows that a proposition is true, they are ordinarily taken to believe it or stand in a closely related affirmative attitude toward it.

Belief alone is not sufficient for knowledge. A person can believe something false, or arrive at a true belief merely by luck.

This is why epistemology studies not only whether a belief is true but whether the subject is in an appropriate epistemic relation to the truth.

== The justified-true-belief analysis ==
A historically influential analysis says that a subject S knows proposition p if and only if:

# p is true;
# S believes p;
# S is justified in believing p.

This is often called the '''justified true belief''' or '''JTB''' analysis.

The three conditions capture important intuitions. False beliefs are not knowledge; knowledge is not usually attributed without some affirmative attitude; and lucky guesses do not seem to count as knowledge.

However, JTB is not generally accepted as a sufficient analysis.

== Gettier cases ==
In 1963 Edmund Gettier presented short cases in which a person appears to have a justified true belief but arrives at the truth through epistemic luck.

Gettier cases triggered a large literature because they suggest that truth, belief, and [[Justification|justification]] can all be present while knowledge is absent.

Proposed responses include:
* adding a "no false lemmas" or defeater condition;
* requiring reliable belief-forming processes;
* requiring safety or sensitivity to truth across nearby possibilities;
* anti-luck conditions;
* treating knowledge as a primitive concept that should not be reduced to a list of more basic conditions.

No single post-Gettier analysis is universally accepted.

== Justification, warrant, and reliability ==
Different epistemological theories explain the relevant positive connection to truth in different ways.

'''Evidentialist''' approaches emphasize whether a belief fits the subject's [[Evidence|evidence]].

'''Reliabilist''' approaches emphasize whether the belief was produced or sustained by a process that tends to produce true beliefs.

'''Internalist''' approaches generally require epistemically relevant factors to be appropriately available to the subject.

'''Externalist''' approaches allow factors outside the subject's reflective access to contribute to knowledge or justification.

Other views emphasize intellectual virtue, proper function, safety, sensitivity, competence, or freedom from defeating evidence.

== Knowledge-first approaches ==
Some contemporary epistemologists argue that attempts to define knowledge in terms of belief plus additional conditions put the explanatory order backwards.

On a '''knowledge-first''' approach, knowledge is treated as a fundamental epistemic concept that can help explain evidence, rational action, assertion, or belief rather than being reduced to them.

This does not make the traditional analysis literature irrelevant; it changes which concept is treated as basic.

== Knowledge and certainty ==
Knowledge should not automatically be equated with certainty. People can know many ordinary facts while acknowledging some possibility of error.

[[Uncertainty]] can concern the evidence, measurement, model, or environment without eliminating all knowledge. Conversely, strong subjective confidence does not by itself create knowledge.

== Social and scientific knowledge ==
Knowledge can be distributed across communities and institutions rather than residing only in one individual.

Scientific knowledge depends on observation, [[Measurement|measurement]], inference, criticism, [[Replication|replication]], instruments, records, expertise, and shared methods. Testimony and division of cognitive labor are therefore central to real knowledge systems.

A community can preserve information that no single member could independently derive, while still facing questions about trust, [[Provenance|provenance]], bias, and error.

== Epistemic notes ==
Knowledge is an established concept but not a settled reductive definition. BITwiki should not state that knowledge simply "is justified true belief." JTB is an important historical analysis whose insufficiency is one of the central lessons of contemporary epistemology.

